    "body": "The Multi-agent collaboration pattern becomes useful when a team can use handoff formats to keep automations clean with clear owners, review paths and metrics. Treat the article like a small operating manual, not a broad thought piece.\n\nLatest news anchor: OpenAI published \"Building self-improving tax agents with Codex\" on May 27, 2026. This draft also checks OpenAI's \"Warp’s big bet on building open source with GPT-5.5\" so the piece is not built from a single headline.\n\n## The operator question\n\nWhat is the smallest workflow that would improve if this signal is true? A good answer names the user, the input, the output, the reviewer and the failure mode.\n\n## Decision table\n\n| Lens | Useful question | Editorial output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Market | What actually changed around Multi-agent collaboration? | Separate source facts from interpretation. |\n| Reader | What decision does the operator need to make? | Give a direct answer before analysis. |\n| Risk | What could be wrong or early? | Mark uncertainty and avoid fake precision. |\n| Action | What is the smallest next step? | Translate the signal into how to use handoff formats to keep automations clean. |\n\n## Build sequence\n\n1. Read the strongest sources from OpenAI, Hugging Face, Anthropic.\n2. Write the direct answer in the first paragraph.\n3. Define one workflow where Multi-agent collaboration changes a decision.\n4. Add a metric that proves whether the change helped.\n5. Update the article when the source landscape shifts.\n\n:::chart\ntitle: Multi-agent collaboration signal radar\nlabels: Source confidence|Market heat|Workflow impact|Execution difficulty\nvalues: 79|84|62|73\ncaption: Relative editorial scores for framing the article, not market sizing or investment advice.\n:::\n\n## Where teams overbuild\n\nThe common mistake is turning every AI trend into a platform project. Most teams need a smaller move: a checklist, a source card, a review rule or a dashboard that helps one decision become clearer.\n\n## Lab judgment\n\nALTOS LAB should publish the playbook only when it can show a reader how to use handoff formats to keep automations clean without hiding uncertainty. The work is useful when the next action is obvious.",
