    "body": "Agent ROI metrics is easy to describe and harder to use. The uncomfortable point: many teams will lose time by reacting to the headline before they know which decision the trend actually changes.\n\nLatest news anchor: OpenAI published \"Building self-improving tax agents with Codex\" on May 27, 2026. This draft also checks OpenAI's \"How Endava builds an agentic organization with Codex\" so the piece is not built from a single headline.\n\n## The common misread\n\nAI markets reward speed, so every update can feel urgent. But urgency is not the same as priority. Agent ROI metrics deserves attention only if it changes a customer expectation, a cost line, a product workflow or a measurable risk.\n\n## What the sources actually support\n\n- OpenAI: Building self-improving tax agents with Codex (May 27, 2026) — See how OpenAI, Thrive, and Crete built a self-improving tax agent with Codex, automating filings, improving…\n- OpenAI: How Endava builds an agentic organization with Codex (May 28, 2026) — Learn how Endava uses Codex to build an agentic organization, accelerating software delivery and reducing…\n- OpenAI: Warp’s big bet on building open source with GPT-5.5 (May 27, 2026) — Warp uses GPT-5.5 and OpenAI models to coordinate coding agents across local, cloud, and open-source…\n- OpenAI: OpenAI for Business\n\n## A sharper way to frame it\n\n| Lens | Useful question | Editorial output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Market | What actually changed around Agent ROI metrics? | Separate source facts from interpretation. |\n| Reader | What decision does the operator need to make? | Give a direct answer before analysis. |\n| Risk | What could be wrong or early? | Mark uncertainty and avoid fake precision. |\n| Action | What is the smallest next step? | Translate the signal into how to move from saved hours to task success and quality cost. |\n\n## Signal chart\n\n:::chart\ntitle: Agent ROI metrics signal radar\nlabels: Source confidence|Market heat|Workflow impact|Execution difficulty\nvalues: 65|70|48|59\ncaption: Relative editorial scores for framing the article, not market sizing or investment advice.\n:::\n\n## The better question\n\nInstead of asking whether to chase Agent ROI metrics, ask what evidence would make the team change behavior this month. If the answer is vague, keep watching. If the answer is concrete, write the small experiment.\n\n## ALTOS LAB point of view\n\nALTOS LAB should sound opinionated without pretending to know more than the sources allow. A strong column names the tradeoff, shows the evidence and leaves the reader with a cleaner judgment.",
