    "body": "AI feature kill criteria is easy to describe and harder to use. The uncomfortable point: many teams will lose time by reacting to the headline before they know which decision the trend actually changes.\n\nLatest news anchor: OpenAI published \"MUFG aims to become AI-native with OpenAI\" on May 28, 2026. This draft also checks OpenAI's \"Warp’s big bet on building open source with GPT-5.5\" so the piece is not built from a single headline.\n\n## The common misread\n\nAI markets reward speed, so every update can feel urgent. But urgency is not the same as priority. AI feature kill criteria deserves attention only if it changes a customer expectation, a cost line, a product workflow or a measurable risk.\n\n## What the sources actually support\n\n- OpenAI: MUFG aims to become AI-native with OpenAI (May 28, 2026) — MUFG uses ChatGPT Enterprise to build an AI-native organization, improve workflows, and deliver new…\n- OpenAI: Warp’s big bet on building open source with GPT-5.5 (May 27, 2026) — Warp uses GPT-5.5 and OpenAI models to coordinate coding agents across local, cloud, and open-source…\n- OpenAI: OpenAI, Grupo Folha and Grupo UOL announce strategic content partnership (May 25, 2026) — OpenAI partners with Grupo Folha and Grupo UOL to bring trusted Brazilian journalism to ChatGPT, expanding…\n- Anthropic: Anthropic Research\n\n## A sharper way to frame it\n\n| Lens | Useful question | Editorial output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Market | What actually changed around AI feature kill criteria? | Separate source facts from interpretation. |\n| Reader | What decision does the operator need to make? | Give a direct answer before analysis. |\n| Risk | What could be wrong or early? | Mark uncertainty and avoid fake precision. |\n| Action | What is the smallest next step? | Translate the signal into how to use three metrics to decide scale or shutdown. |\n\n## Signal chart\n\n:::chart\ntitle: AI feature kill criteria signal radar\nlabels: Source confidence|Market heat|Workflow impact|Execution difficulty\nvalues: 82|87|65|76\ncaption: Relative editorial scores for framing the article, not market sizing or investment advice.\n:::\n\n## The better question\n\nInstead of asking whether to chase AI feature kill criteria, ask what evidence would make the team change behavior this month. If the answer is vague, keep watching. If the answer is concrete, write the small experiment.\n\n## ALTOS LAB point of view\n\nALTOS LAB should sound opinionated without pretending to know more than the sources allow. A strong column names the tradeoff, shows the evidence and leaves the reader with a cleaner judgment.",
